      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_kGHc-Ic742hcBParSFRaIwjl8S93iHa5xfQ1eaYfrcNg=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "It’s an interesting but of trivia but  don’t think comparing annual tourist visits to resident population reflects whether a place feels dominated by tourism. You need to look at the peak daily visitor numbers, and also consider the spatial spread.\n\nTo illustrate that, I live in London which is a top tourist destination globally. Apparently we get nearly 40m visitors each year (75% of them are international) to our city of 9m; annual visitors even outnumber the population of our city region by tens of millions. But as a resident the vast majority of the time I don’t feel like I’m living in some sort of massive museum or resort or whatever. That’s probably because there are never more than 1.5m visitors on even the busiest of days. And even then, aside from a handful of relatively small areas, it seems like any other city filled with residents of that city getting on with their daily lives.",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-24T12:35:13Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
